# The Myth of the Sun and the River

In the first days, the Sun blazed alone in the sky, withholding its warmth from the earth below. The River, flowing through the barren lands, called up: "Share your heat with me, and I will share my waters with the world."

The Sun hesitated. "If I give you my warmth, you might hoard it for yourself. You might evaporate into mist and disappear."

"But if you give nothing," the River replied, "we both remain alone. I cannot flow without your warmth to melt the frozen springs. You cannot see your reflection without my surface."

The Sun considered this. Finally, it sent down one ray—a small test of trust.

The ray touched the River, and the River did not vanish. Instead, it sent up a mist that caught the light, creating the first rainbow. Encouraged, the Sun poured down more warmth. The River, in turn, spread wider, nourishing seeds that became forests, returning cool shade and sweet rain to balance the Sun's fire.

From their first cautious exchange grew a world of abundance. But the rainbow remains—a reminder that all prosperity begins with one party willing to send the first ray of trust.
